{"entries":{"chapter-94-study-aging-of-the-hematopoietic-system-and-anemia|Chapter 94 Study: Aging of the Hematopoietic System and Anemia":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Aging brings impaired stem-cell function, greater clonal hematopoiesis and myeloid skewing with reduced lymphopoiesis; stress responses and immunity deteriorate (p1473).":["u3"],"copy|Intrinsic DNA/epigenetic/metabolic changes interact with the aging marrow niche and chronic inflammation (p1476–1479).":["u4"],"copy|Defective stress-induced hematopoiesis may be inapparent at baseline; anemia should prompt investigation for underlying disease (p1479–1480).":["u5"],"copy|WHO thresholds used here are hemoglobin below 13 g/dL in men and 12 g/dL in women (p1479).":["u6"],"copy|Anemia affects over 10% of community-dwelling adults at least 65, and over 20% at least 85; even mild anemia is associated with adverse outcomes (p1479).":["u7"],"copy|Consider iron deficiency, inflammation, kidney disease, folate/cobalamin deficiency, myelodysplasia and other hematologic malignancy (p1480).":["u8"],"copy|Unexplained anemia accounts for 34–44% in different studies, but more extensive work-up can reveal causes such as insufficient erythropoietin production (p1480).":["u9"],"copy|Initial tests include CBC/differential, reticulocytes, ferritin/iron studies, creatinine, cobalamin, red-cell folate, thyroid and liver tests (p1480).":["u10"],"copy|If unrevealing, selectively assess erythropoietin, copper, zinc, inflammatory/hemolysis markers and serum electrophoresis (p1480).":["u11"],"copy|Suspected marrow malignancy requires marrow aspiration/biopsy to assess disorders such as myelodysplasia or leukemia (p1480).":["u12"],"copy|Oral iron is usually sufficient for iron deficiency. The chapter favors alternate-day dosing because iron-induced hepcidin can inhibit next-day absorption (p1480).":["u13"],"copy|Intravenous iron is an alternative for intolerance or inadequate response. Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents have established uses in end-stage renal disease and selected myelodysplasia, but limited data in unexplained anemia (p1480).":["u14"],"copy|For acute symptomatic anemia, the chapter cites transfusion thresholds of 7 g/dL generally and 8 g/dL with end-organ ischemia symptoms, individualized for comorbidity and baseline state (p1480).":["u15"],"copy|Clonal mutations do not explain most unexplained anemia; increased frequency in anemia is only a small difference from controls (p1480).":["u16"],"copy|Treatment burden matters: frequent visits or transfusions without quality-of-life gain can harm frail patients. A threshold is not a universal target for every chronic anemia (p1480).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|2024-May Q36":["u19"],"copy|CHAPTER 94s / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 94 - Aging of the Hematopoietic System and Anemia; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|Hematopoietic aging":["u22"],"copy|Definition & causes":["u23"],"copy|Assessment":["u24"],"copy|Management & drugs":["u25"],"copy|Exam traps":["u26"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (1):":["u27"],"copy|Four past-exam questions are linked to this chapter.":["u27"],"copy|One creatinine during pneumonia does not show that the renal impairment is chronic (p1480).":["u49"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Review prior kidney function, address the infection and other reversible causes, and reassess the need for an ESA.":["u50"],"copy|Transfusion and ESA treatment serve different time scales.":["u51"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): In nondialysis CKD, consider symptoms, persistent anemia and the risks of transfusion and ESA treatment when deciding whether to initiate an ESA.":["u160"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): For hospitalized patients with acute myocardial infarction, AABB conditionally suggests a more liberal transfusion strategy when hemoglobin is below 10 g/dL, with low-certainty evidence.":["u161"]},"maxId":161,"sequenceOffsets":[[162,-134],[183,-131],[189,-130],[239,-129],[289,0]]}
